<Read
  slug="the-last-switch"
  description={"Since 1950 nuclear weapons have crashed, burned, fallen and sunk. None is on record as having gone off, and the reason, again and again, was the last safeguard still standing. The record, from the government lists that publish it, starts over Canada. Three things Canada can do this year."}
  standfirst={"Since 1950, nuclear weapons have crashed, burned, fallen and sunk, and none is on record as having gone off. Read how close each came, and that stops being a comfort. The record starts over Canada."}
>
      <section class="brief" aria-labelledby="in-brief">
        <h2 id="in-brief">In brief</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The United States counted 32 accidents with its own nuclear weapons between 1950 and 1980, and published the list in 1981. None made a nuclear explosion. In many, the conventional explosive inside the bomb went off.</li>
          <li>The first one on that list happened off British Columbia. Nine months later, a bomb’s explosive charge went off 760 metres above the St. Lawrence. Canadians were told it was a conventional dump, and learned the truth in 2000.</li>
          <li>In 1961 a hydrogen bomb fell on North Carolina with three of its four safeguards gone. The engineer who studied it wrote that one switch “stood between the United States and a major catastrophe.” Part of the other bomb is still under the field.</li>
          <li>Some of these weapons were never found. Two lie off the American east coast, one off Georgia, one off Okinawa, and dozens of Soviet warheads on the floor of the Atlantic and the Pacific.</li>
          <li>Six times, a false alarm or a misread signal put a launch decision within reach, and each time a person read it right.</li>
          <li>Canada built the first reactor outside the United States, used it to treat cancer, sent the last American warheads home in 1984, and wrote the mandate for the treaty that would stop the world making bomb material. Three things Canada can do this year are at the end.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <h3>How to read this</h3>
      <p>Every event here is taken from a government record where one exists: the American list of 1981, the Sandia laboratory’s own histories, the International Atomic Energy Agency’s inventory of losses at sea, and Canada’s and Britain’s published records. Where sources disagree, the page says so and uses the one written at the time. Every source is at the end.</p>
      <p>It is written for anyone who has been told these weapons are safe, and for the people in Ottawa who could make them less necessary.</p>
      <p class="byline">Northern Temper. Drafted with the research in the open, 1 November 2026. <a href="#sources">The receipts are at the end.</a></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">01 · Over Canada first</span> The record starts here.</h2>
      <p>On 13 February 1950, an American B-36 bomber flying from Alaska to Texas lost three engines to ice over the Pacific. The crew dropped its bomb into the sea off British Columbia and bailed out over Princess Royal Island. The government’s own summary reads: “A bright flash occurred on impact.” Only the high explosive went off. The bomb carried a dummy core.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup></p>
      <p>It is the first entry on the list.</p>
      <p>On 10 November 1950, a B-50 in trouble jettisoned a Mark IV atomic bomb over the St. Lawrence, near Rivière-du-Loup. Its nuclear core was not aboard. Its high explosive was, and it went off 760 metres above the middle of the river, loud enough to shake both shores.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a>, <a href="#note-3">3</a></sup></p>
      <p>Canadians were told three conventional bombs had been dumped. The truth came out in 2000, in a letter from Defence Minister Art Eggleton to a Bloc Québécois MP, Paul Crête, who had asked.<sup><a href="#note-4">4</a></sup> The American list still records the event as “Over Water, outside United States,” because it names no foreign place but two.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup></p>
      <p>There may be a third. The IAEA’s inventory lists an American B-36 lost off Newfoundland on 18 March 1953, with a footnote: the United States “has neither confirmed nor denied the presence of nuclear material.”<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></p>
      <p class="pull">The first nuclear weapon accident on record happened in Canadian waters. The second was over the St. Lawrence.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">02 · What counts</span> Thirty-two accidents, by the government’s own count.</h2>
      <p>The United States did something most nuclear powers never have. In 1981 its Department of Defense published plain summaries of 32 accidents with its nuclear weapons, from 1950 to 1980. That is why most of this paper is American. Not because the United States was more careless, but because it said so in writing.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup></p>
      <p>Its definition is broad: a weapon launched or fired by accident, a nuclear or conventional explosion, a burning weapon, contamination, or a weapon lost, stolen or jettisoned. Its finding is clear: “There never has been even a partial inadvertant U.S. nuclear detonation.” Every “detonation” in the list was conventional explosive only.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup></p>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>32</dt><dd>Accidents with American nuclear weapons, 1950 to 1980, in the government’s own published list.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup></dd></div>
        <div><dt>0</dt><dd>Nuclear explosions among them. In the words of the laboratory that designs the safety, none “produced any measurable nuclear yield.”<sup><a href="#note-2">2</a></sup></dd></div>
        <div><dt>1</dt><dd>Switch, in 1961, between a hydrogen bomb on the ground in North Carolina and its explosion.<sup><a href="#note-2">2</a>, <a href="#note-7">7</a></sup></dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p>Read the summaries and the zero stops sounding like a guarantee. At Palomares, Spain, in 1966, a bomber and its tanker collided. The explosive in two of four hydrogen bombs went off on impact and spread plutonium over 2.26 square kilometres around the village. About 1,400 tons of soil were shipped to the United States, and in 2015 the United States agreed to finish the job.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a>, <a href="#note-12">12</a></sup></p>
      <p>At Thule, Greenland, in 1968, a B-52 with four weapons aboard caught fire and crashed on the sea ice seven miles from the runway. All four were destroyed. Crews removed 237,000 cubic feet of ice, snow and water. A Danish study, reported by the National Security Archive, says one uranium component was never found.<sup><a href="#note-8">8</a>, <a href="#note-12">12</a></sup></p>
      <p>At Mars Bluff, South Carolina, in 1958, a navigator grabbed the emergency release while securing a bomb. It fell 15,000 feet into the Gregg family’s garden. The explosive went off, and the house was torn to shreds. Nobody died.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a>, <a href="#note-11">11</a></sup></p>
      <p>At Damascus, Arkansas, in 1980, a worker dropped a wrench socket inside a Titan II missile silo. It caused a leak in a fuel tank. Eight and a half hours later the silo exploded and threw the missile’s nine-megaton warhead, by published estimates, out of the ground. It landed about 100 feet from the gate and released nothing. One airman, David Livingston, was killed.<sup><a href="#note-9">9</a>, <a href="#note-13">13</a></sup></p>
      <p>The list ends in 1980. The record does not. In 2007, a B-52 flew six cruise missiles with real nuclear warheads 1,400 miles across the United States, because they had been loaded in place of dummies. They were never armed. A senator put it this way: “No one knew where they were or even missed them for over 36 hours.”<sup><a href="#note-16">16</a></sup></p>
      <p>And 32 is a definition, not a total. One serious fire, a hydrogen bomb engulfed in burning fuel at RAF Lakenheath in England in January 1961, is not on the list at all. The journalist Eric Schlosser found it in documents released to him, along with a 1967 Defense Department compilation of about 1,000 accidents and incidents from July 1957 to March 1967 alone.<sup><a href="#note-17">17</a></sup></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">03 · The switch</span> Four safeguards. One held.</h2>
      <p>On 24 January 1961, a B-52 on airborne alert over North Carolina broke up when its right wing failed. Two Mark 39 hydrogen bombs fell out as it broke apart, each of three to four megatons by published estimates. Three of the eight crew died.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a>, <a href="#note-9">9</a></sup></p>
      <p>The first bomb’s parachute opened, and it came down nearly intact. Its firing sequence had started on the way down. Sandia’s own history says what stopped it: “The nonoperation of the cockpit-controlled ready-safe switch prevented nuclear detonation of this bomb.”<sup><a href="#note-2">2</a>, <a href="#note-6">6</a></sup></p>
