      <p>Parker Jones, a Sandia engineer, reviewed the accident in 1969 and wrote the sentence that made Goldsboro famous when the memo was released in 2013: “One simple, dynamo-technology, low voltage switch stood between the United States and a major catastrophe!”<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a></sup></p>
      <p>You may have read that five of six safeguards failed. That figure is wrong, and Jones said so in the same memo. The bomb had four. One held.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a>, <a href="#note-8">8</a></sup></p>
      <p>The second bomb’s parachutes did not open. It hit a muddy field and broke apart, and nothing exploded. Crews dug 50 feet down and could not recover the part that holds the uranium. The Air Force bought an easement on the land, so nobody may dig there without permission.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a>, <a href="#note-2">2</a>, <a href="#note-6">6</a></sup></p>
      <p class="pull">The record is not that the bombs are safe. It is that the last switch held.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">04 · Still out there</span> Some were never found.</h2>
      <p>The IAEA counts “seven recorded accidents” at sea that ended in the confirmed loss of one or more nuclear weapons, American and Soviet.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup> Add the ones lost near land and the list below is what the published record still cannot account for.</p>
      <section class="tw" tabindex="0" aria-label="Figure 2, weapons never recovered, scrolls sideways">
        <table class="keyed">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th> <th scope="col">Where</th> <th scope="col">What is still there</th></tr></thead>
