      <p class="fig">Figure 2 · Weapons and nuclear parts never recovered, from the published record. A Soviet submarine lost in the Bay of Biscay in 1970, K-8, also went down with nuclear warheads; the count was never published.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></p>
      <p>On K-219, a seaman named Sergey Preminin stayed behind to shut the reactors down. He did not get out.<sup><a href="#note-15">15</a></sup></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">05 · The wrong signal</span> Six times, a person read it right.</h2>
      <p>Accidents are what the machines do. The near misses are what the warnings do, and they come down to a person at a desk with minutes to decide.</p>
      <figure class="timeline" aria-labelledby="fig-3">
        <figcaption id="fig-3" class="fig">Figure 3 · Six false alarms and misread signals, 1962 to 1995</figcaption>
        <ol>
          <li><p class="yr">1962</p><p>Off Cuba, American ships drop practice depth charges to tell a Soviet submarine to surface. Its captain, Valentin Savitsky, believes he is under attack and orders a nuclear torpedo prepared. Vasili Arkhipov, the brigade’s chief of staff, sees that the Americans are signalling and calms him. The order is never passed on.<sup><a href="#note-18">18</a></sup></p></li>
          <li><p class="yr">1979</p><p>Screens at NORAD show 1,400 Soviet missiles in flight. A training tape has been loaded into a live computer. About a dozen interceptors take off. The alarm is cleared in about six minutes.<sup><a href="#note-19">19</a></sup></p></li>
          <li><p class="yr">1980</p><p>The screens show it again, twice in one week. The cause is a failed computer chip that cost 46 cents. Bomber crews run to their aircraft and start their engines.<sup><a href="#note-19">19</a></sup></p></li>
          <li><p class="yr">1983</p><p>A Soviet satellite reports five American missiles launched. It has mistaken sunlight on high cloud for rocket plumes. Lieutenant Colonel Stanislav Petrov judges it false and tells his commanders so.<sup><a href="#note-20">20</a></sup></p></li>
          <li><p class="yr">1983</p><p>A NATO exercise, Able Archer, rehearses the release of nuclear weapons, and parts of the Soviet forces go to an unusual alert. A later American review found the United States “may have inadvertently placed our relations with the Soviet Union on a hair trigger.”<sup><a href="#note-21">21</a></sup></p></li>
          <li class="now"><p class="yr">1995</p><p>Norway launches a research rocket to study the northern lights. It had told its neighbours, but the notice never reached the right Russian officials. President Yeltsin has his nuclear briefcase opened, the only time that is known to have happened.<sup><a href="#note-22">22</a></sup></p></li>
        </ol>
      </figure>
      <p class="pull">Every one of them ended with a person reading the screen right. The record does not say what happens the time someone reads it wrong.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">06 · What the record proves</span> One in a million is a promise about one accident.</h2>
      <p>The engineers did remarkable work, and the record shows it. Since 1968 American weapons have been held to a written standard. The chance of a nuclear explosion in a crash or fire must not exceed one in a million “per warhead exposure or accident.” If the explosive is set off at any single point, the chance of more than four pounds’ worth of nuclear yield must not exceed one in a million.<sup><a href="#note-23">23</a></sup></p>
      <p>Those are odds for one event. The record is a count of how many events there have been, and the count only grows for as long as the weapons exist. In 1990 a panel of three physicists told Congress that the warhead on the Trident submarine missile, the W88, had been designed without the safer kind of explosive and sits close to a rocket motor whose fuel can detonate. The Federation of American Scientists reports that the warhead’s current refit replaces the explosive with the safer kind.<sup><a href="#note-23">23</a></sup> That is the system working, more than thirty years after the warning.</p>
      <p>And the American list is the long one only because it was published. Britain released one table, of seven accidents and twelve incidents, and says none released radioactive material. In 2009 a British and a French submarine, both carrying nuclear missiles, collided under the Atlantic. No French list could be found. Nothing from China could be found at all.<sup><a href="#note-24">24</a></sup></p>
      <p>On 5 February 2026, the last treaty limiting the two largest arsenals, New START, expired. It left, in the words of the Associated Press, no caps on either. Russia says it will keep to the old limits as long as the United States does.<sup><a href="#note-25">25</a></sup></p>
      <p>There is a hard answer to all of this, and it belongs here rather than in a critic’s reply. When the Soviet Union broke up, Ukraine held the third-largest nuclear arsenal in the world. In December 1994 it gave the weapons up, and Russia, the United States and Britain signed a memorandum promising “to respect the independence and sovereignty and the existing borders of Ukraine.”<sup><a href="#note-36">36</a></sup> Twenty years later Russia took Crimea, and many people now read the lesson as: keep your weapons.</p>
      <p class="view"><strong>Our view:</strong> every safeguard in this paper did its job, and every one of them was a probability. A probability is not a promise, and Ukraine’s lesson is that a promise is not enough either. What is left is a count anyone can check. The treaty Canada wrote and the checking Canada pays for are exactly that, and section 08 asks for more of both.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">07 · What Canada does</span> Canada split the atom and treated cancer with it.</h2>
      <p>Canada was in this from the start. The Montreal Laboratory worked on the Manhattan Project, and Canada supplied and refined uranium for the American plants. On 5 September 1945, at Chalk River, ZEEP became “the first controlled nuclear chain reaction constructed outside of the United States.”<sup><a href="#note-26">26</a>, <a href="#note-27">27</a></sup></p>
      <p>Then Canada chose. The Canadian Nuclear Safety Commission puts it in one line: “Canada was the first country with significant nuclear capability to reject nuclear weapons.”<sup><a href="#note-26">26</a></sup></p>
      <p>On 27 October 1951, at Victoria Hospital in London, Ontario, a team under Ivan Smith gave the world’s first cobalt-60 cancer treatment. Twelve days later in Saskatoon, Harold Johns’s team gave the first with a precisely measured dose, to a mother of four who lived another 47 years. The machines the two cities built have treated millions.<sup><a href="#note-28">28</a></sup> For decades, the NRU reactor at Chalk River supplied 30 to 40 percent of the world’s medical isotopes, and at its peak as much as 80.<sup><a href="#note-27">27</a></sup></p>
      <p>The ledger has a second column, and it belongs on the same page.</p>
      <section class="tw" tabindex="0" aria-label="Figure 4, Canada's record, scrolls sideways">
