      <p class="fig">Figure 4 · Canada’s record, both columns. The safety commission’s fact sheet that makes the “first country” claim records the uranium and the plutonium too.<sup><a href="#note-26">26</a></sup></p>
      <p>Canada also stands half the watch. The shortest way from Eurasia to North America runs over the pole and down across this country, and 47 of the 50 North Warning radars are on Canadian soil.<sup><a href="#note-34">34</a></sup> Sixteen of the 337 stations that listen for nuclear tests around the world are here too.<sup><a href="#note-31">31</a></sup></p>
      <p>Ottawa’s position on the newest ban, the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons, is in its own words: Canada is “not a signatory,” because the treaty “is incompatible with our NATO commitments,” and it “shares the underlying concerns about the very slow progress of disarmament.”<sup><a href="#note-33">33</a></sup> Everything below can be done by a NATO member, this year, under any government.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">08 · What to do</span> Three things Canada can do this year.</h2>
      <ol class="asks">
        <li><h3>Open Canada’s own file.</h3><p>The United States published its accidents in 1981. Canada learned about the bomb over the St. Lawrence from a minister’s letter in 2000. National Defence should publish every record it holds of an accident or incident with a nuclear weapon in Canadian territory, waters or airspace, from 1950 to 1984, and ask Washington to confirm or deny the 1953 loss off Newfoundland.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Finish the treaty Canada wrote.</h3><p>Canada wrote the mandate in 1995 and chaired the group that agreed on its contents in 2018. The Conference on Disarmament works by consensus and has not started. Canada has done this before: the landmine ban was negotiated outside that conference, under Canadian leadership, and signed in Ottawa in December 1997.<sup><a href="#note-35">35</a></sup> Table the 2018 report as a text, and invite the countries that will negotiate.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Pay for the checking.</h3><p>A world with fewer weapons only happens if the count can be checked. Canada already listens for tests from Yellowknife and has shown Chalk River’s methods for proving a warhead is gone. It has given $2.5 million to that work since 2017.<sup><a href="#note-32">32</a></sup> Multiply it, and offer Chalk River as the place the methods are proven.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="view"><strong>Our view:</strong> none of this needs a lecture to anyone. It is what Canada already does, done further. A country that turned the first reactor outside the United States into a cancer machine has the standing to ask the world to count its warheads, and the instruments to do the counting.</p>

      <p class="pull">Every safeguard in this paper held. The way to keep that record is to need fewer of them.</p>
