      <p>November 2026</p>

      <h2 id="words"><span class="eyebrow">Words used here</span> Seven terms, plainly.</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>Broken Arrow</dt>
        <dd>The American military’s flag word for an accident with a nuclear weapon. The 1981 list itself never uses it; it says “accident.”<sup><a href="#note-17">17</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>Core, capsule or pit</dt>
        <dd>The nuclear material at the centre of a weapon. Early bombs carried it separately, as a “capsule,” and many of the 1950s accidents involved bombs with the capsule out.</dd>
        <dt>High explosive</dt>
        <dd>The conventional explosive packed around the core. In a weapon it must fire perfectly evenly to make a nuclear explosion. In an accident it can go off on its own, which destroys the bomb and can scatter its plutonium.</dd>
        <dt>Secondary</dt>
        <dd>The second stage of a hydrogen bomb, which holds uranium and supplies most of its power. The part never recovered at Goldsboro.</dd>
        <dt>One-point safe</dt>
        <dd>The rule that setting off the explosive at any single point must give, at most, a one in a million chance of more than four pounds’ worth of nuclear yield.<sup><a href="#note-23">23</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>Fissile material</dt>
        <dd>The plutonium and highly enriched uranium a bomb is made of. The treaty Canada proposed in 1995 would stop the world making more of it.</dd>
        <dt>Verification</dt>
        <dd>Checking, by measurement rather than trust, that a country has done what it says: stopped testing, stopped producing, or taken a warhead apart.</dd>
      </dl>

